Subject: [PATCH 10267/11362] Rationale for the worktree import guard (content
 landed in 45b9e83f47)

The guard's three files were staged and the conductor's concurrent `git add -A`
committed them under its own message before this session's commit ran. Content
is intact; the reasoning was lost. Recorded here rather than left out, because a
change nobody can explain later is a change that gets undone later. This is also
mechanism 3 -- the shared index -- demonstrating itself while the commit message
describing it was being written.

WHAT LANDED

Concurrent agents sharing one checkout destroyed each other's work six distinct
ways on 2026-08-29: pre-commit's stash window losing and resurrecting edits, a
shared index swallowing another agent's staged paths, `git commit -- <paths>`
publishing vandalised worktree content, a stash race re-applying a mutation over
its restore, and two narrow scopes deadlocking.

Per-agent git worktrees close all six. Each agent gets its own working directory
and index over a shared object store, so there is no common worktree to stash and
no common index to collide in. It needs no repository change -- the Agent tool
takes `isolation: "worktree"` -- which is why the change was a guard rather than
a mechanism.

The guard is the precondition. The venv resolves `import carnot` to the MAIN
checkout regardless of cwd, so a worktree run tests code the agent did not edit.
In a fresh worktree, `carnot.__file__` pointed at the main checkout. An edit made
in the worktree stayed invisible until PYTHONPATH was pinned; pinning made it
visible immediately. Both directions were run before writing the guard.

Unguarded, worktrees would trade a loud recoverable failure for a silent one:
every assertion passing and every mutation proof reading GREEN while measuring
the wrong tree. That is strictly worse than the problem being fixed.

So tests/python/conftest.py refuses at collection when the tests and the carnot
package come from different checkouts, naming the exact PYTHONPATH that fixes it.
Fail-closed: a refused run costs a minute with the fix in the message, an allowed
one costs a green verdict about a file nobody edited.

Verified three ways: worktree unpinned refuses, worktree pinned passes 11/11,
main checkout unaffected 11/11. Four mutations RED (containment always sound;
string-prefix instead of path containment; override on any truthy value; fix
dropped from the message), restore byte-identical, 7/7 green after.

The escape hatch is exact-match "1" on CARNOT_ALLOW_FOREIGN_CARNOT_IMPORT, so a
stray truthy value cannot silently disable it on the run that matters.

REQ-INFRA-6810.

